"Artonans summon Avowed away from Earth to quests."
"Most summons last a few weeks; longer ones are extremely rare."
"An Avowed who does not come back from a summons is presumed dead."
"A summons can yank an Avowed away at any moment."
"Summons come from members of the Artonan wizarding classes, for emergencies or not."
"Artonans can summon Avowed from anywhere and do not care where they live."
"A summons notice names the world, says whether a refusal is available, and counts down."
"Emergency summons are instantaneous, and answering them is rewarded accordingly."
"A refusal can be used even late, after a summons is scheduled."
"The return drop never splices or endangers the Avowed, but may land them among onlookers."
"Summoners can let Avowed walk instead of teleporting, sometimes for a small bonus."
"Each wizard has an individual System credit limit on summons and teleports."
"The System suggests whom to summon; the summoner decides, and summoning is not free."
"A primary summoner has precedence and can cancel other summoners' quests."
"Contract-facilitated summons are reserved for the elite among the elite."
"One big mistake, such as an Avowed dying off-Contract, usually bars a wizard for life."
"Beginner summoners are more dangerous to Avowed than advanced ones."
"Artonan students get provisional Contract access after several years of school."
"Competent graduates with recommendations may summon low-rank Avowed without oversight."
